diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** No subsystem, daemon, interface, or policy is faulty. The Campus 1 multilayer switch (mswitch0) inter-VLAN routing between VLAN 10 and VLAN 30 is operating correctly.

**Mechanism:** All three SVIs on mswitch0 are UP and properly configured as 802.1Q VLAN sub-interfaces on top of bridge br0: vlan10@br0 (172.16.10.254/24, VLAN ID 10), vlan20@br0 (172.16.20.254/24, VLAN ID 20), and vlan30@br0 (172.16.30.254/24, VLAN ID 30). Bridge VLAN filtering is disabled (vlan_filtering=0) so all tagged traffic flows freely. IP forwarding is enabled, the kernel routing table has direct connected routes for all three subnets, and mswitch0's ARP table is fully populated for hosts in all three VLANs. The bridge FDB correctly maps VLAN 30 host MACs (pc12/13/14) to eth4 (switch3 uplink) and VLAN 10 host MACs to eth2 (switch1 uplink).

**Localization:** The downstream switches (switch1, switch3) are also properly configured with per-VLAN sub-interfaces (eth0.10, eth0.30) bridging VLAN-tagged trunk traffic from mswitch0 to their untagged access ports.

**Root cause:** There is no fault. The reported symptom (pc5 cannot reach VLAN 30) is unconfirmed — a direct probe from pc5 to 172.16.30.12 returns 0% packet loss at 1.3ms (TTL=63), and the topology-wide reachability sweep shows no loss on any pair. The initial apparent "timeout" was a measurement artifact: three chained `ping -c4` commands take approximately 12 seconds on a healthy link, exceeding the diagnostic tool's 10-second execution limit.

**Fix:** No corrective action required. The inter-VLAN routing infrastructure on mswitch0 is fully functional. If the user continues to report issues, verify the end-host's default gateway configuration and ARP cache.
